/-!
# Generators of the ideal-tensor kernel after localized base change

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 00MO, with Tags 00MM, 00MN and 00M6.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-localized-base-change-ideal-kernel-span)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w z t

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Module

/-- The multiplication kernel after localized base change is generated by the original kernel.
Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 00MO, proof, second and third paragraphs;
Tags 00MM, 00MN and the naturality of the ideal-kernel description in Tag 00M6.
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-localized-base-change-ideal-kernel-span)

Proof:
1. Set A = S tensor_R R', N = R' tensor_R M, Q = R/I, J = IR' and Q' = R'/J.
   Tensor cancellation identifies M' = S' tensor_S M with W-inverse N over A.
   Write K = ker(I tensor_R M to M) and K_N = ker(J tensor_R' N to N).
2. The quotient map R to Q' factors through Q. Tag 00MM, using the assumed
   flatness of Q tensor_R M, gives a surjection
   Tor_1^R(M,Q) tensor_Q Q' to Tor_1^R(M,Q'). Tag 00MN then gives a surjection
   from the latter to Tor_1^R'(N,Q'). Under the connecting-map descriptions of
   Tag 00M6, these identify with a surjection K tensor_R R' to K_N.
   Indeed I annihilates K: it annihilates Tor_1^R(M,Q) through its second variable,
   and the connecting map is R-linear. The quotient tensor relations therefore
   identify K tensor_R R' with K tensor_Q Q'. Naturality of connecting maps
   sends k tensor r' to r' times the image of k in J tensor_R' N.
3. Multiplication J tensor_R' N to N is A-linear. Exactness of localization
   identifies W-inverse K_N with the multiplication kernel for M'. Compatibility
   of tensor products with localization identifies W-inverse (J tensor_R' N)
   with J tensor_R' M', and carries each multiplication map to multiplication.
   Localization of the surjection in step 2 is still onto this kernel.
4. Its values on generators are algebraMap(R', S')(r') times theta(k).
   This follows on a tensor a tensor x from the displayed formula for theta,
   and on arbitrary tensors by additive tensor induction. Fractions have the
   same form with an additional inverse of q(w), a scalar in S'. Thus every
   element of the localized multiplication kernel is an S'-linear combination
   of theta(k), k in K. Tensor symmetry puts the S'-module factor on the left,
   so the conclusion is the displayed membership in the S'-span.

No local-ring or Noetherian hypotheses, no finiteness, and no vanishing assumption
on theta are used. For I = 0 or I = R the original and resulting kernels vanish;
localization at a set containing zero gives the zero module as well. -/
theorem mem_span_ideal_kernel_localized_baseChange
    {R : Type u} {S : Type v} {R' : Type w} {S' : Type z}
    [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [CommRing R'] [CommRing S']
    [Algebra R S] [Algebra R R'] [Algebra S S'] [Algebra R' S'] [Algebra R S']
    [IsScalarTower R S S'] [IsScalarTower R R' S']
    (I : Ideal R)
    (M : Type t) [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M] [Module R M]
    [IsScalarTower R S M]
    (hloc : ∃ (q : S ⊗[R] R' →+* S') (W : Submonoid (S ⊗[R] R')),
      (∀ (s : S) (r' : R'), q (s ⊗ₜ[R] r') =
        algebraMap S S' s * algebraMap R' S' r') ∧
      (letI := q.toAlgebra; IsLocalization W S'))
    (hquot : Module.Flat (R ⧸ I) ((R ⧸ I) ⊗[R] M))
    (θ : I ⊗[R] M →+ (I.map (algebraMap R R')) ⊗[R'] (S' ⊗[S] M))
    (hθ : ∀ (a : I) (x : M), θ (a ⊗ₜ[R] x) =
      (⟨algebraMap R R' a, Ideal.mem_map_of_mem (algebraMap R R') a.property⟩ :
        I.map (algebraMap R R')) ⊗ₜ[R'] ((1 : S') ⊗ₜ[S] x))
    (z : (I.map (algebraMap R R')) ⊗[R'] (S' ⊗[S] M))
    (hz : TensorProduct.lift
      ((LinearMap.lsmul R' (S' ⊗[S] M)).comp (I.map (algebraMap R R')).subtype) z = 0) :
    TensorProduct.comm R' (I.map (algebraMap R R')) (S' ⊗[S] M) z ∈
      Submodule.span S'
        ((fun k : I ⊗[R] M ↦
          TensorProduct.comm R' (I.map (algebraMap R R')) (S' ⊗[S] M) (θ k)) ''
          (LinearMap.ker (TensorProduct.lift ((LinearMap.lsmul R M).comp I.subtype)) :
            Set (I ⊗[R] M))) := by
  sorry

end Module
